{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"plan","namespace":"","artifact_type":"","name":"P999000ed"},"owner_reference":"#3978","content":"{\"plan_id\":\"P999000ed\",\"feature\":\"primary-quota-403-retryable\",\"version\":\"1.0.0\",\"description\":\"\",\"goal\":\"Classify GitHub primary-quota rate-limit refusals as retryable with a bounded reset-derived retry_after on every REST and GraphQL path; align probe_backend_status with the same classifier (A); bound PyGithub's in-transport primary-limit wait (B); populate ViewItemResult.number (C); remove dead ViewItemResultCompact; keep permission-denied 403s, GraphQL refusals and secondary limits unchanged.\",\"context\":\"# Plan context: #3978 primary-quota 403 retryable\\n\\nInputs every task reads itself: `artifact_read(item_id=3978, artifact_type=\\\"architect\\\")` (spec, sections cited per task as 'spec \\u00a7N'), `artifact_read(item_id=3978, artifact_type=\\\"feature-context\\\")`, and `backlog_view(selector=\\\"#3978\\\", summary=False, sections=[\\\"Acceptance Criteria\\\"])` (12 ACs).\\n\\n## Test runner (verified 2026-09-28)\\nRun from the repo root: `uv run --locked --script plugins/development-harness/run_pytests.py <paths relative to plugin root> -q`. A bare `uv run pytest` from the repo root fails on this plugin's conftest import (`tests.network_blocked`). Loopback sockets are allowed by the plugin conftest network guard; all other network is blocked.\\n\\n## Planning decisions (auto mode)\\n- D1 reset-delay boundary: `rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds` returns `min((reset - now) + 1.0, 3900.0)`, and returns `None` when that value is <= 0, or when the header is absent, empty, non-numeric or non-finite. Consequence: reset == now gives 1.0; reset 1s or more in the past gives None. This settles the spec \\u00a76 'confirm exact boundary' note and the spec \\u00a72 Q1 / \\u00a74.1 wording mismatch.\\n- D2 (orchestrator): `requests.exceptions.RetryError` as retryable (spec \\u00a73.3) is its own task (T2) so review can judge it separately.\\n- D3 (orchestrator): a test pins PyGithub `GithubRetry().secondary_rate_wait == 60` and urllib3 `Retry.DEFAULT_BACKOFF_MAX == 120` as installed, and that the \\u00a79 threshold stays above both (T5). Observed values on 2026-09-28: 60 and 120.\\n- D4 (orchestrator): delete dead `ViewItemResultCompact` only after a search proves no reference (T7). This supersedes spec \\u00a77's Non-Goal on that class.\\n- D5 (orchestrator): Issue C is reproduce-first (T6 step 1 records failing output).\\n- D6: tests are written before or alongside each fix (TDD). Every task names its exact verification command.\\n\\n## Planning assumptions (unknown-frontier closures)\\n- U1 (GraphQL delay path in or out of scope): closed by spec \\u00a74.2a and AC9. In scope, T3.\\n- U2 (live primary-limit responses carry `x-ratelimit-reset` behind proxies): not verifiable offline, and running the live e2e lane locally against the production backlog is forbidden (AGENTS.md). Assumption: PyGithub passes response headers through unmodified (spec \\u00a73.2, Requester.createException). When the header is missing, the fix falls back to `retry_after=None` plus the sync engine's fixed 30/120s floor (spec Q1). No task. Residual risk is recorded for TN.\\n\\n## File ownership (same-file conflicts serialized by dependencies)\\n- sync_state.py: T1 then T2. tests/test_sync_state_classify.py: T1 then T2.\\n- gh_client.py: T3 only (GraphQL fallback and probe_backend_status merged).\\n- github_client.py and backlog_core/tests/test_github_client.py: T4 only. T5 writes a new file.\\n- parsing.py: T6. models.py: T7. backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md: T8.\\n- sync_engine.py, server.py and backends/github_backend.py are verify-only (T9). No task edits them.\\n\\n## Sync checkpoints\\n- SC1 (after T1): the classifier and delay helper are green, AC1-AC4 tests pass, and AC5-AC7 suites pass unmodified. Gates T2, T3 and T4.\\n- SC2 (after T2-T7): every structured-criterion suite passes, and `git diff` shows no edits to the verify-only files. Gates T8 and T9. Reflect: do the classifier (T1/T2), probe_backend_status (T3) and bounded retry (T4) agree on one rate-limit definition?\\n- SC3 (TN): baseline-versus-final comparison of the structured criteria.\",\"acceptance_criteria\":\"\",\"issue\":\"3978\",\"tasks\":[{\"id\":\"T0\",\"title\":\"T0: Capture baseline state\",\"status\":\"not-started\",\"agent\":\"t0-baseline-capture\",\"dependencies\":[],\"blocked_by\":[],\"parallelize_with\":[],\"priority\":1,\"complexity\":\"low\",\"skills\":[\"python-engineering:python3-testing\"],\"created\":\"2026-09-28T10:10:08.407349+00:00\",\"started\":null,\"completed\":null,\"last_activity\":null,\"body\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"analysis_method\":\"none\",\"divergence_notes\":0,\"accuracy_risk\":\"low\",\"is_bookend\":true,\"bookend_type\":\"t0-baseline\"},{\"id\":\"T1\",\"title\":\"sync_state: classify primary-quota 403 retryable and derive retry_after from x-ratelimit-reset\",\"status\":\"not-started\",\"agent\":\"python-engineering:python-cli-architect\",\"dependencies\":[\"T0\"],\"blocked_by\":[],\"parallelize_with\":[\"T6\",\"T7\"],\"priority\":1,\"complexity\":\"high\",\"skills\":[\"python-engineering:python3-testing\"],\"created\":\"2026-09-28T10:11:36.765222+00:00\",\"started\":null,\"completed\":null,\"last_activity\":null,\"body\":\"## Inputs\\n- Spec \\u00a72 (Q1, Q3, Q4, Q5), \\u00a74.1, \\u00a75 and \\u00a76 via `artifact_read(item_id=3978, artifact_type=\\\"architect\\\")`.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py` (`_classify_github_exception` ~351-379, `parse_retry_after_header`/`retry_after_seconds` ~412-460, `RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS` ~49, `__all__` ~57).\\n- Precedent: `gh_client._is_rate_limited` (~892-916) and PyGithub `GithubRetry.increment` (the installed `github/GithubRetry.py`).\\n\\n## CoVe Checks\\n- Claims to verify: (1) PyGithub assigns `RateLimitExceededException` only when `isRateLimitError` is true; (2) PyGithub lowercases header names on `GithubException.headers`; (3) GitHub's primary message starts with 'API rate limit exceeded'; (4) the frozen-clock tests actually control the clock the helper reads.\\n- Questions: Does `Requester.createException` in the installed `github/Requester.py` gate `RateLimitExceededException` on `isRateLimitError`? Does a test with `X-RateLimit-Reset` (mixed case) pass, proving the case-insensitive lookup? Does `str(None) == \\\"0\\\"` stay False for header-less exceptions, as the AC5 fixture requires? Does removing the monkeypatch make the exact-value tests fail (proving the freeze works)?\\n- Evidence: read the installed PyGithub source; pytest output.\\n- Revision rule: when any answer contradicts the spec, implement to the observed source behavior, and state the divergence in the handoff.\",\"description\":\"\",\"objective\":\"A 403 carrying any primary-quota signal classifies RETRYABLE, and `retry_after_seconds` returns a bounded delay derived from x-ratelimit-reset on 403/429 when Retry-After is absent. As a result the sync loop retries instead of going OFFLINE, and MCP errors report `retryable: true` with a `retry_after`.\",\"requirements\":\"### Tests first (TDD)\\n1. Write the failing tests below before editing production code. Record the red run output for the completion report.\\n2. `tests/test_retryable_classification.py`: add parametrized cases, each 403 with no Retry-After, asserting `retryable: true` and a positive `retry_after` at the MCP-response boundary (AC1, AC4): (a) `RateLimitExceededException(403, {\\\"message\\\": \\\"API rate limit exceeded for ...\\\"}, {\\\"x-ratelimit-remaining\\\": \\\"0\\\", \\\"x-ratelimit-reset\\\": <future epoch>})`; (b) a plain `GithubException` with the same message (signal 2 only); (c) a plain `GithubException(403, <unparseable body>, {\\\"x-ratelimit-remaining\\\": \\\"0\\\", \\\"x-ratelimit-reset\\\": <future>})` (signal 3 only).\\n3. `backlog_core/tests/test_rate_limit_retry_after.py`: add `rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds` cases with a frozen clock (monkeypatch the time source the helper reads; never assert against the real wall clock): reset=now+600 gives 601.0; reset=now gives 1.0; reset=now-1 gives None; reset=now-3600 gives None; header missing, `None` headers, `\\\"\\\"`, `\\\"abc\\\"`, `\\\"nan\\\"` and `\\\"inf\\\"` give None; reset=now+10**9 gives 3900.0; header-name casing (`X-RateLimit-Reset`) matches. Add `retry_after_seconds` cases: Retry-After wins when both headers are present (Q5); the reset fallback applies to 429 as well as 403 (Q4); a non-403/429 status gets no reset fallback (AC2).\\n4. `tests/test_sync_state_classify.py`: one case where a primary-limit `RateLimitExceededException` wrapped in `ContentUnavailableError` classifies RETRYABLE through the existing cause-chain walk (spec \\u00a76).\\n5. `backlog_core/tests/test_startup_sync.py`: one integration-style case (AC3). A primary-limit 403 raised inside `_attempt_sync` leaves `SyncStatus` not OFFLINE and sets `state.retry_after` to the reset-derived value. Use the existing `_attempt_sync` fixtures in that file.\\n\\n### Production change (`backlog_core/sync_state.py`)\\n6. Add `_PRIMARY_RATE_LIMIT_MESSAGE_PREFIX`, `_RATE_LIMIT_RESET_HEADER`, `_RATE_LIMIT_REMAINING_HEADER`, `_RATE_LIMIT_RESET_SKEW_SECONDS = 1.0` and `_MAX_RATE_LIMIT_RESET_DELAY_SECONDS = 3900.0` as `Final` constants with docstrings, per spec \\u00a74.1.\\n7. Add `_is_primary_rate_limit_signal(exc, message)`, true when any of the three signals holds (spec Q3), and OR it into the existing 403 `looks_retryable` expression. Keep the existing `has_retry_after` and secondary-marker terms unchanged.\\n8. Add public `rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds(headers)` implementing D1. Header lookup is case-insensitive, reusing the lookup approach of `parse_retry_after_header`. The function never raises on bad input.\\n9. In `retry_after_seconds`'s GithubException 403/429 branch, consult the new helper only when `parse_retry_after_header` returned None.\\n10. Add `\\\"rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds\\\"` to `__all__`. It is not there today, despite spec \\u00a74.1 saying 'already present'.\\n11. Update `classify_sync_error`'s classification-table docstring (~lines 504-543) with the primary-limit-signal bullet and the reset-delay fallback.\",\"constraints\":\"- `sync_state.py` must not import `gh_client` (the one-way `gh_client -> sync_state` direction).\\n- Do not reorder or alter the secondary-limit branch, the 5xx/429 rules, or any existing test assertion. AC5-AC7 suites must pass unmodified.\\n- Do not add `RetryError` handling (that is T2), and do not touch `gh_client.py`, `github_client.py`, `sync_engine.py`, `server.py` or `backends/`.\\n- No new Pydantic models, exception classes or schema changes.\\n- `git add` only the Expected Outputs. No `--no-verify`. No `Fixes/Closes/Resolves #N`.\",\"expected_outputs\":\"- plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py\\n- plugins/development-harness/tests/test_retryable_classification.py\\n- plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_rate_limit_retry_after.py\\n- plugins/development-harness/tests/test_sync_state_classify.py\\n- plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_startup_sync.py\",\"acceptance_criteria\":\"### Classification (AC1, AC4, AC5-AC7)\\n1. Each of the three primary-limit signals alone makes a 403 classify RETRYABLE, and the MCP verdict reports `retryable: true` with a positive `retry_after`.\\n2. `GithubException(403, \\\"Forbidden\\\", {})` stays NON_RETRYABLE. `test_startup_sync.py::test_403_without_retry_after_is_non_retryable`, `test_sync_state_secondary_rate_limit.py` and `test_sync_state_graphql_refusal.py` pass with zero diff to those tests.\\n### Delay (AC2)\\n3. Every D1 boundary case in Requirement 3 passes with the exact value listed. No case returns a negative number or a value above 3900.0.\\n4. Retry-After precedence and 429 coverage are pinned by tests.\\n### Sync loop (AC3)\\n5. The `_attempt_sync` case asserts status is not OFFLINE and `retry_after` equals the reset-derived delay.\\n### Hygiene\\n6. The red run was recorded before implementation. `__all__` exports the helper. The docstring table names the new rule.\",\"verification_steps\":\"1. Red: `uv run --locked --script plugins/development-harness/run_pytests.py tests/test_retryable_classification.py backlog_core/tests/test_rate_limit_retry_after.py tests/test_sync_state_classify.py backlog_core/tests/test_startup_sync.py -q` fails on the new cases before the production edit. Save the output.\\n2. Green: `uv run --locked --script plugins/development-harness/run_pytests.py tests/test_retryable_classification.py backlog_core/tests/test_rate_limit_retry_after.py tests/test_sync_state_classify.py backlog_core/tests/test_startup_sync.py tests/test_sync_state_secondary_rate_limit.py tests/test_sync_state_graphql_refusal.py backlog_core/tests/test_graphql_rate_limit_detection.py -q` passes.\\n3. `git diff --stat -- plugins/development-harness/tests/test_sync_state_secondary_rate_limit.py plugins/development-harness/tests/test_sync_state_graphql_refusal.py` is empty. `git diff plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_startup_sync.py` shows additions only.\\n4. `uv run ruff check plugins/development-harness/backlog_core && uv run ty check plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py`\\n5. `grep -n \\\"gh_client\\\" plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py` shows no import.\\n6. `git add plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py plugins/development-harness/tests/test_retryable_classification.py plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_rate_limit_retry_after.py plugins/development-harness/tests/test_sync_state_classify.py plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_startup_sync.py && git commit -m \\\"fix(backlog-core): classify primary-quota 403 retryable with reset-derived delay\\\"`\",\"context_notes\":\"Merged from two planned changes to one file (`sync_state.py`): (a) the 403 primary-limit classification signal and (b) the shared x-ratelimit-reset delay helper. They are one task because AC4 (MCP `retryable: true` with a `retry_after`) needs both. Spec \\u00a72 Q1/Q3/Q4/Q5, \\u00a74.1, \\u00a75 and the \\u00a76 rows for the test files below. Plan-context decision D1 fixes the delay boundary.\",\"handoff\":\"Report: the red and green outputs, the D1 values observed, confirmation that the AC5-AC7 test files have zero diff, and the commit SHA. Name any consumer whose behavior differed from spec \\u00a74.3.\",\"analysis_method\":\"none\",\"divergence_notes\":0,\"accuracy_risk\":\"high\",\"reason\":\"Primary edit site. Every consumer (sync engine, MCP verdict, backends, gh_client) routes through these two functions. T2, T3 and T4 build on it.\"},{\"id\":\"T2\",\"title\":\"sync_state: classify requests RetryError (exhausted transport retry budget) as retryable\",\"status\":\"not-started\",\"agent\":\"python-engineering:python-cli-architect\",\"dependencies\":[\"T0\",\"T1\"],\"blocked_by\":[],\"parallelize_with\":[\"T3\",\"T4\",\"T6\",\"T7\"],\"priority\":2,\"complexity\":\"low\",\"skills\":[\"python-engineering:python3-testing\"],\"created\":\"2026-09-28T10:12:48.102604+00:00\",\"started\":null,\"completed\":null,\"last_activity\":null,\"body\":\"## Inputs\\n- Spec \\u00a73.3 and \\u00a74.1 (the `RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS` block).\\n- Installed sources: `requests/adapters.py` (MaxRetryError to RetryError), `requests/exceptions.py`, and `github/GithubRetry.py` (`except (MaxRetryError, GithubException): raise`).\\n\\n## CoVe Checks\\n- Claims: (1) `RetryError` is a subclass of `OSError`; (2) `HTTPAdapter.send` raises `RetryError` when `MaxRetryError.reason` is a `ResponseError`; (3) `classify_sync_error` checks `RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS` before its generic OSError branch.\\n- Questions: Does `uv run python -c \\\"import requests; print(issubclass(requests.exceptions.RetryError, OSError))\\\"` print True? Does the installed `requests/adapters.py` contain the `isinstance(e.reason, ResponseError)` -> `RetryError` branch? Does placing the entry in the tuple actually change the verdict, or does an earlier branch intercept it (confirm with the red/green test)?\\n- Revision rule: when the branch order prevents the tuple from taking effect, stop and report BLOCKED with the observed order, rather than restructuring `classify_sync_error`.\",\"description\":\"\",\"objective\":\"`classify_sync_error(requests.exceptions.RetryError(...))` returns RETRYABLE through `RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS`. No other exception's verdict changes.\",\"requirements\":\"1. Test first: in `tests/test_sync_state_classify.py`, add a case asserting `requests.exceptions.RetryError(\\\"...\\\")` classifies RETRYABLE, and that `retry_after_seconds` on it returns None (no headers, per spec \\u00a73.3 and Q1 'no fabrication'). Record the red run.\\n2. Add `requests.exceptions.RetryError` to `RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS` (`backlog_core/sync_state.py` ~49-55), with a comment citing spec \\u00a73.3.\\n3. Add a `RetryError` bullet to `classify_sync_error`'s classification-table docstring.\\n4. Confirm that the tuple's other consumers (`grep -rn RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS plugins/development-harness/backlog_core`) are not changed in unintended ways, and list each consumer in the handoff.\",\"constraints\":\"- Only the tuple entry, its comment, the docstring bullet and the one test. No other edits to `sync_state.py`.\\n- Do not fabricate a `retry_after` for RetryError.\\n- `git add` only the Expected Outputs. No `--no-verify`. No `Fixes/Closes/Resolves #N`.\",\"expected_outputs\":\"- plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py\\n- plugins/development-harness/tests/test_sync_state_classify.py\",\"acceptance_criteria\":\"1. The new RetryError test fails before the edit and passes after it.\\n2. `retry_after_seconds(RetryError(...))` is None.\\n3. Every other case in `tests/test_sync_state_classify.py` and `backlog_core/tests/test_startup_sync.py` still passes. The completion report lists every consumer of `RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS` and whether its behavior changed.\\n4. The completion report explicitly flags this as the spec \\u00a73.3 addition beyond the item's ACs, for separate review.\",\"verification_steps\":\"1. `uv run --locked --script plugins/development-harness/run_pytests.py tests/test_sync_state_classify.py backlog_core/tests/test_startup_sync.py backlog_core/tests/test_rate_limit_retry_after.py tests/test_retryable_classification.py -q` (red before the edit, green after).\\n2. `grep -rn \\\"RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS\\\" plugins/development-harness/backlog_core`\\n3. `uv run ruff check plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py && uv run ty check plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py`\\n4. `git add plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py plugins/development-harness/tests/test_sync_state_classify.py && git commit -m \\\"fix(backlog-core): treat exhausted-retry RetryError as retryable\\\"`\",\"context_notes\":\"Spec \\u00a73.3: when PyGithub's `GithubRetry` exhausts `total`, urllib3 raises `MaxRetryError`, which requests converts to `requests.exceptions.RetryError` (an `OSError`, not a `GithubException`). `classify_sync_error` currently sends it to the generic OSError branch, which returns NON_RETRYABLE. This change is additive to `RETRYABLE_TRANSIENT_EXCEPTIONS`. It runs after T1 because both tasks edit `sync_state.py` and `tests/test_sync_state_classify.py`.\",\"handoff\":\"Report the red and green output, the consumer list from step 2 with a per-consumer impact note, the CoVe answers, and the commit SHA.\",\"analysis_method\":\"none\",\"divergence_notes\":0,\"accuracy_risk\":\"medium\",\"reason\":\"Orchestrator decision D2: spec \\u00a73.3 goes beyond the item's original ACs, so it gets a separately reviewable task and commit.\"},{\"id\":\"T3\",\"title\":\"gh_client: GraphQL reset-derived retry_after and probe_backend_status reuses classify_github_failure\",\"status\":\"not-started\",\"agent\":\"python-engineering:python-cli-architect\",\"dependencies\":[\"T0\",\"T1\"],\"blocked_by\":[],\"parallelize_with\":[\"T2\",\"T4\",\"T6\",\"T7\"],\"priority\":2,\"complexity\":\"medium\",\"skills\":[\"python-engineering:python3-testing\"],\"created\":\"2026-09-28T10:14:05.632505+00:00\",\"started\":null,\"completed\":null,\"last_activity\":null,\"body\":\"## Inputs\\n- Spec \\u00a74.2 and \\u00a78 (artifact `architect`, item 3978).\\n- `backlog_core/gh_client.py` (~76 import, ~892-916 `_is_rate_limited`, ~960-975 `_graphql_request`, ~2008-2050 `probe_backend_status`).\\n- `backlog_core/sync_state.py` after T1 (`rate_limit_reset_delay_seconds`, `classify_github_failure`).\\n\\n## CoVe Checks\\n- Claims: (1) `classify_github_failure` dispatches a `GithubException` to `_classify_github_exception`; (2) inside the `status == 403` guard, only the three 403 signals or Retry-After can yield RETRYABLE; (3) consumers of `BackendStatus` handle `BackendAvailability.ERROR` from this branch.\\n- Questions: Does `classify_github_failure` in `sync_state.py` (~463-475) reach `_classify_github_exception` for a bare `GithubException`? Does any consumer of `probe_backend_status` (grep the callers) special-case RATE_LIMITED in a way that the ERROR result for permission 403s would break? Does `GithubException(403, \\\"Forbidden\\\", {})` in the new test actually reach the issue-count `except` branch rather than the earlier `repo_obj is None` branch?\\n- Revision rule: when a consumer breaks on ERROR, report it in the handoff with file:line, and do not widen scope to that consumer.\",\"description\":\"\",\"objective\":\"A GraphQL primary-limit refusal with only x-ratelimit-reset raises `GitHubRateLimitedError` with a positive bounded `retry_after`. `probe_backend_status` reports RATE_LIMITED only for 403s that `classify_github_failure` marks RETRYABLE, and reports ERROR for a plain per